  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nMain threat today will be flash flooding from heavy rain producing\nthunderstorms. Satellite showed the rather thick mid level cloud\ndeck over the mountains and I-25 Corridor is thinning, allowing \nfor stronger solar insolation and warming. Observed ACARS \nsoundings show that we've nearly broken the cap as of 1 pm. In \nfact, we're now seeing towering cumulus over the mountains and \neven moderate cumulus over portions of the plains. In addition, \none could argue a weak shortwave (in the form of a departing right\nentrance region of an upper level jet branch) was riding up \nthrough central/north central Colorado. Finally, weak pressure \nrises are occurring over the northeast plains which means a more \neasterly upslope low level component is expected just prior to \nstorm initiation. Therefore, all ingredients are in place for  \nscattered to numerous storms this afternoon, pushing eastward \nacross the plains through this evening. There could even be a \nround or two of weaker (but still briefly heavy) storms behind the\ninitial stronger storms. For more hydro details about the threat \nof flash flooding, see the HYDROLOGY section of this Area \nForecast Discussion below.\n\nWe can't rule out an isolated severe storm as MLCAPE grows to \n800-1400 J/kg over the plains, with a wet microburst being the \nmain threat and just an isolated marginal hail threat in this \nmonsoonal, heavy rain environment. \n\nFriday's weather is shaping up to be quite similar to today's.\nWe'll still be under the deeper monsoonal moisture plume although\nthere are signals for slight drying and warming. However, that \ncould very well be offset by the increase in boundary layer\nmoisture and 60F plus dewpoints and patchy fog/stratus tonight.\nWhile there is no discernible shortwave at this point, we still\nexpect a minimally capped environment. That will allow scattered \nto numerous storms to fire over the mountains by early afternoon \nand spread east across the plains late in the afternoon and \nevening. See Hydrology discussion again for more details for \nFriday's heavy rain/flash flood threat.\n\nSaturday and Sunday are also shaping up to be active weather days.\nWhile there is slight drying noted as flow aloft turns slightly\nmore W-SW, we'll likely see daily shortwaves as we'll be in the\nfavorable right entrance region of an upper level jet. Bulk shear\nwill also be increasing again which means a greater risk of a\ncouple severe storms. Temperatures will remain below seasonal\nnormals by several degrees with potential for backdoor cool\nfronts. Highs on the plains are expected to average in the lower\n80s.  \n\nFor early next week, there is still good agreement for a drier \nwesterly component as the ridge begins to amplify over the \nCentral/Southern Rockies. That will support less storm coverage\nstarting Monday, and temperatures warming to or just above \nseasonal norms. There's more uncertainty for later next week \nwhether we return to monsoonal southwest flow or stay drier and \nmore westerly.",
